---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// The comprehensive MaxBars reference — FlatBars' flagship surface: most of FullBars,
// reinterpreted, plus infix operators, a pipe, and bare loop variables. Structured deltas-first
// (operators → pipes → the identity → loop vars), Jinja-style around an
// expression core rather than re-walking the Handlebars spine — then the inherited
// FullBars constructs (shown only with operators woven through, linking to
// /fullbars rather than re-teaching), the divergences, compile, and a "when (not)
// to use" close. Prose is illustrative only; every runnable example comes from the
// single gate-validated source ../maxbars.mjs and renders through the real
// `maxbars` engine. The ~80-helper prelude is NOT re-listed (the generated catalog
// owns that) — this page teaches the expression layer and links the catalog.
import Reference from "../layouts/Reference.astro";
import SurfaceDeltas from "../components/SurfaceDeltas.astro";
import OpenInLab from "../components/OpenInLab.jsx";
import { examples as ex } from "../maxbars.mjs";

const SPEC_BASE = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_SPEC_BASE || "/flatbars/spec/";

// Sticky left-nav: MaxBars concepts, then the sibling surfaces, then the Lab.
const groups = [
  {
    label: "MaxBars",
    items: [
      { href: "#intro", label: "What MaxBars is" },
      { href: "#reference", label: "Surface reference" },
      { href: "#operators", label: "Operators" },
      { href: "#pipes", label: "Pipes" },
      { href: "#identity", label: "The identity" },
      { href: "#loops", label: "Loop variables" },
      { href: "#inherited", label: "From FullBars" },
      { href: "#together", label: "Putting it together" },
      { href: "#divergences", label: "Where it diverges" },
      { href: "#compile", label: "Compiles to JS" },
      { href: "#when", label: "When (not) to use" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "New here?",
    items: [{ href: "/start", label: "Getting started" }],
  },
  {
    label: "Other surfaces",
    items: [
      { href: "/minbars", label: "MinBars — Mustache" },
      { href: "/rawbars", label: "RawBars — the core" },
      { href: "/fullbars", label: "FullBars — Handlebars" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Play",
    items: [{ href: "/lab/index.html?engine=maxbars", label: "FlatBars Lab ↗" }],
  },
];

// Inline tag references — kept in a helper so Astro never parses the {{ }}.
const t = (s) => s;
---

<Reference
  title="MaxBars — the FlatBars expression language"
  subtitle="MaxBars is FlatBars' flagship surface — an expression layer over the same meaning-free core: infix operators ({{ a >= b }}), a pipe ({{ value | helper }}), and bare loop variables. The most capable surface in the family; it borrows most of FullBars and adds the expression freedom FullBars holds back — and underneath, every operator is still just a helper application."
  groups={groups}
  spec="maxbars"
  dialect="maxbars"
>
  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Intro ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intro">
    <h2>What MaxBars is</h2>
    <p>
      <b>MaxBars is FlatBars' flagship surface — an expression layer over the same
      meaning-free core.</b> If you've read the <a href="/fullbars">FullBars reference</a>,
      you'll recognise most of it: MaxBars borrows the bulk of FullBars and adds three
      things on top (a few FullBars-only constructs are the deliberate exceptions —
      see <a href="#divergences">where MaxBars and FullBars diverge</a>):
    </p>
    <ul class="conv">
      <li><b>Infix operators</b> — <code>{t("{{score >= 50}}")}</code>, <code>{t("{{price * qty}}")}</code>, <code>{t("{{a && b}}")}</code> — instead of nesting subexpressions.</li>
      <li><b>A pipe</b> — <code>{t("{{value | helper}}")}</code> — to read transformations left-to-right.</li>
      <li><b>Bare loop variables</b> — <code>{t("index1")}</code>, <code>{t("first")}</code>, <code>{t("length")}</code> — inside <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      None of it is new machinery. <code>{t("{{price * qty}}")}</code> <em>desugars to</em>
      <code>{t("(multiply price qty)")}</code> — the same helper application FullBars and
      RawBars run. MaxBars is sugar that compiles down the ladder, not a second engine.
    </p>
    <p>The simplest template — the panes are live, so start here:</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.hello.template} data={ex.hello.data} />
    <div class="callout info">
      <p>
        <b>Where MaxBars sits.</b> It's the most permissive surface in the family —
        expressions live in the template. If you want strict model-view separation
        (the StringTemplate philosophy — no logic in templates), drop down the ladder:
        <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a> is the same engine with none of this sugar, and
        <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a> sits in between. One core, four levels of
        expressiveness.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────── Surface reference ───────────────────── -->
  <section id="reference">
    <h2>Surface reference</h2>
    <p>The syntax and value-policy deltas that define MaxBars, at a glance — generated from the
      same single source (<code>shared/surface-syntax.json</code>) the FlatBars Lab's Reference
      popup and the spec engine page read, so it can never drift from what they render.</p>
    <SurfaceDeltas surface="maxbars" />
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────── Operators ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="operators">
    <h2>Operators</h2>
    <p>
      Every operator is infix sugar for a prelude helper. Arithmetic is <b>strictly
      numeric</b> — there is no string <code>+</code> (that throws). Comparisons are
      non-associative (no <code>{t("a < b < c")}</code> chains). Group with parentheses
      to control precedence.
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Operators</th><th>Desugars to</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="code">+ - * / %</span></td><td><span class="code">add subtract multiply divide modulo</span></td><td><span class="code">{t("{{price * qty}}")}</span> → <span class="code">12</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">== != &lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;=</span></td><td><span class="code">eq ne lt gt lte gte</span></td><td><span class="code">{t("{{qty >= 1}}")}</span> → <span class="code">true</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">&amp;&amp; || !</span></td><td><span class="code">and or not</span></td><td><span class="code">{t("{{a && !b}}")}</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">??</span></td><td><span class="code">coalesce</span></td><td><span class="code">{t("{{nick ?? name}}")}</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">?:</span></td><td><span class="code">firstTruthy</span></td><td><span class="code">{t("{{nick ?: name}}")}</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">{t("? :")}</span></td><td><span class="code">ternary</span></td><td><span class="code">{t("{{ok ? yes : no}}")}</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3>Comparisons in conditions</h3>
    <p>
      The everyday use: a comparison as the head of <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code>. Where
      FullBars writes the subexpression <code>{t("{{#if (gte stock 10)}}")}</code>, MaxBars
      writes the infix form — and chains with <code>{t("{{else if …}}")}</code> and
      <code>{t("{{#unless}}")}</code>. (Arithmetic gets its own showcase in
      <a href="#identity">the identity</a> below.) The one catch: a clause separator
      <em>must</em> parenthesise its condition (<code>{t("{{else if (gte stock 1)}}")}</code>) —
      see <a href="#divergences">where it diverges</a>.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.conditionals.template} data={ex.conditionals.data} />
    <h3>Logic, <code>??</code>, and <code>?:</code></h3>
    <p>
      <code>&amp;&amp;</code> binds tighter than <code>||</code> and looser than the
      comparisons; parenthesise when in doubt. The three are <b>distinct</b>, and reaching
      for the wrong one is the classic trap — <code>||</code> is the <em>boolean</em>
      operator (it answers “is either truthy?”), so <code>{t("{{nickname || name}}")}</code>
      outputs the text <code>true</code>, not a name. To <em>fall back to a value</em>,
      use a coalesce:
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Answers</th><th>Yields</th><th><code>{t("{{nickname OP name}}")}</code>, <code>nickname: ""</code></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="code">||</span></td><td>is either <em>truthy</em>?</td><td>a boolean</td><td><span class="code">Hi true</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">??</span></td><td>is the left <em>present</em>? (non-null)</td><td>a value</td><td><span class="code">Hi </span> (keeps the <code>""</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">?:</span></td><td>is the left <em>truthy</em>?</td><td>a value</td><td><span class="code">Hi Ada</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      So <code>??</code> only substitutes on null/undefined (an empty string or <code>0</code>
      on the left is kept), while <code>?:</code> (Elvis) substitutes whenever the left is
      falsy under MaxBars’ rule — the operator you want for a blank-or-missing fallback.
      A boolean <code>||</code>/<code>&amp;&amp;</code> left in output position is flagged by
      <code>flatbars lint --maxbars</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ternary <code>{t("cond ? a : b")}</code> is the third coalescing form — it chooses
      between <b>two different</b> values on a separate condition (the inline
      <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code>), desugaring to <code>ternary cond a b</code>. The
      comparison binds tighter than <code>?</code>, and it is right-associative. All three —
      <code>??</code>, <code>?:</code>, and the ternary — in one example:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.defaults.template} data={ex.defaults.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────── Pipes ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="pipes">
    <h2>Pipes</h2>
    <p>
      The pipe <code>|</code> feeds the value on its left in as the helper's
      <b>first argument</b>: <code>{t("{{name | uppercase}}")}</code> is exactly
      <code>{t("(uppercase name)")}</code>. Any further arguments follow the helper
      name, so <code>{t("{{price | toFixed 2}}")}</code> is <code>{t("(toFixed price 2)")}</code>.
      Pipes <b>chain</b> left-to-right, each stage feeding the next — the readable way to
      express a sequence of transforms that would otherwise nest inside-out — and the
      right of a pipe can be <code>{t("lookup")}</code>, the idiom for FullBars's
      <code>{t("{{uppercase (lookup …)}}")}</code> subexpression. One example, four pipes:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.pipes.template} data={ex.pipes.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      The right-hand side of a pipe is a helper from the
      <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}engine/prelude/`}>prelude catalog</a> (here <code>pluck</code>,
      <code>join</code>, <code>uppercase</code>, <code>toFixed</code>) — or one a host
      registers. The pipe doesn't add helpers; it's a second way to call them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────── The identity: op ⇄ helper ⇄ subexpr ────────────── -->
  <section id="identity">
    <h2>The identity: operator ⇄ helper ⇄ subexpression</h2>
    <p>
      This is the idea the whole family rests on. An infix operator, a named helper
      call, and a parenthesised subexpression are <b>the same operation written three
      ways</b> — they parse to the identical core application and render identically.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.identity.template} data={ex.identity.data} />
    <p>
      So "MaxBars vs FullBars vs RawBars" isn't three engines — it's how much sugar the
      surface gives you over one core. <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a> shows the bottom of
      that ladder, where <code>{t("(multiply price qty)")}</code> is all there is.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────────── Loop variables ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="loops">
    <h2>Loop variables</h2>
    <p>
      Inside <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code>, MaxBars exposes the loop state as
      <b>bare variables</b> — no sigil, just names you apply like any other helper.
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="code">{t("index0")}</span> / <span class="code">{t("index1")}</span></td><td>position, 0-based / 1-based</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">{t("rindex0")}</span> / <span class="code">{t("rindex1")}</span></td><td>remaining, 0-based / 1-based</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">{t("first")}</span> / <span class="code">{t("last")}</span></td><td><span class="code">true</span> on the first / last element</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">{t("length")}</span></td><td>the collection's length</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">{t("key")}</span></td><td>the property name when iterating an object</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The state is reached under <code>{t("loop.")}</code> — <code>{t("{{loop.index0}}")}</code>,
      <code>{t("{{loop.last}}")}</code>. Context climbs with <code>{t("parent")}</code> (the enclosing
      context, chainable as <code>{t("parent.parent.x")}</code>) and <code>{t("root")}</code> (the
      top-level data) — there is no <code>{t("@")}</code> and no <code>{t("../")}</code> in MaxBars
      (<code>{t("@root")}</code> → <code>{t("root")}</code>, <code>{t("../x")}</code> → <code>{t("parent.x")}</code>);
      an <code>{t("@")}</code>-name is a parse error. A nested loop shows it all at once:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.eachList.template} data={ex.eachList.data} />
    <h3>Over an object</h3>
    <p>Iterating an object, <code>{t("loop.key")}</code> is the property name and <code>{t("{{this}}")}</code> the value.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.eachObject.template} data={ex.eachObject.data} />
    <h3>Ranges — the <code>..</code> operator</h3>
    <p>
      <code>{t("a..b")}</code> is the inclusive integer range (sugar for the <code>range</code> helper),
      so <code>{t("{{#each 1..n}}")}</code> counts without a data array. The bounds are expressions and
      additive binds tighter than <code>..</code> (<code>{t("start..start + 2")}</code>); descending bounds
      yield the empty list. A single <code>.</code> is still a dotted path or decimal —
      only a glued <code>..</code> is the range operator.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.rangeOp.template} data={ex.rangeOp.data} />
    <h3>Collection literals — <code>[list]</code> &amp; <code>&#123;dict&#125;</code></h3>
    <p>
      <code>{t("[a, b, c]")}</code> is a list literal (sugar for the <code>list</code> helper) and
      <code>{t("{k: v}")}</code> a dict (sugar for <code>dict</code>). Elements and values are full
      expressions, so they nest and take infix — <code>{t("[1, n + 1, {tag: x}]")}</code>. Keys are
      bare identifiers or quoted strings; an empty <code>{t("[]")}</code>/<code>{t("{}")}</code> is
      falsy under the <code>nonEmpty</code> rule. The structural scanner is brace-aware, so a dict
      needs no space before the tag close — <code>{t("{{#with {a: 1}}}")}</code> reads cleanly (a
      <code>{t("}}")}</code> inside a dict-value string is not a tag close either).
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.collections.template} data={ex.collections.data} />
    <h3>Let — block-scoped aliases</h3>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{#let name=value}}…{{/let}}")}</code> binds template-local constants for the body — a
      deep path, a computed value, a small table — that vanish at <code>{t("{{/let}}")}</code>. Unlike
      <code>{t("{{#with}}")}</code> it <b>never re-roots</b>: a bare <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code> still
      resolves against the current context, so <code>let</code> only adds aliases. Bindings are
      <b>sequential</b> — a later value sees an earlier name (<code>{t("tax=(multiply subtotal rate)")}</code>
      after <code>{t("subtotal=…")}</code>). A binding whose name matches a helper shadows it for the body.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.letBindings.template} data={ex.letBindings.data} />
    <h3>Block params &amp; labelled loops</h3>
    <p>
      A trailing <code>{t("as elem index index1")}</code> binds scoped names over the block body —
      <b>drop the pipes</b>: MaxBars writes <code>{t("as p i")}</code>, not the Handlebars
      <code>{t("as |p i|")}</code> (a bar in a block head is a parse error here). MaxBars binds a
      <em>third</em> name to the 1-based index (<code>{t("as item i j")}</code>), one more than
      <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a>'s two. The names stay in scope inside nested blocks and reach an
      outer <em>element</em>, but not the outer loop's <code>{t("index")}</code> /
      <code>{t("length")}</code>. A trailing <code>{t("label NAME")}</code> clause (ADR-013, MaxBars-only)
      names the loop <b>frame</b>, so an inner loop reads <code>{t("outer.index1")}</code>,
      <code>{t("outer.length")}</code>, <code>{t("outer.last")}</code> of the enclosing loop. (To pipe a
      head argument, parenthesise it — <code>{t("{{#each (rows | reverse) as r}}")}</code>.)
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.loopParams.template} data={ex.loopParams.data} />
    <h3>The empty case</h3>
    <p><code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> carries its own <code>{t("{{else}}")}</code> for an empty list — inherited from FullBars.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.eachElse.template} data={ex.eachElse.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Inherited from FullBars ─────────────────── -->
  <section id="inherited">
    <h2>Most of FullBars — with operators inside</h2>
    <p>
      MaxBars borrows FullBars's core underneath, so paths (<code>{t("user.address.city")}</code>,
      <code>{t("../x")}</code>, <code>{t("@root")}</code>), the block helpers
      <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code> / <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> / <code>{t("{{#with}}")}</code> /
      <code>{t("{{#unless}}")}</code>, escaping, and subexpressions all work as the
      <a href="/fullbars">FullBars reference</a> documents them. The headline addition is that
      operators and pipes can now appear anywhere a value can — and a handful of FullBars-only
      constructs are the deliberate exceptions (see <a href="#divergences">where it diverges</a>).
      The everyday basics carry over unchanged — a dotted path, a missing key rendering empty,
      and comments:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.dotted.template} data={ex.dotted.data} />
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.missing.template} data={ex.missing.data} />
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.comment.template} data={ex.comment.data} />
    <h3><code>with</code> — re-root the context</h3>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.withBlock.template} data={ex.withBlock.data} />
    <h3>Escaping</h3>
    <p><code>{t("{{x}}")}</code> HTML-escapes; the triple-stash <code>{t("{{{x}}}")}</code> emits raw markup — unchanged from FullBars.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.escaping.template} data={ex.escaping.data} />

    <h3>Raw blocks</h3>
    <p>
      A <b>raw block</b> — four staches, <code>{t("{{{{#op}}}}…{{{{/op}}}}")}</code> (MaxBars
      uses the <code>#</code> sigil, like RawBars) — hands its body to the operation
      <b>unprocessed</b>: the inner <code>{t("{{bar}}")}</code> is never interpreted,
      it's literal text the operation receives via <code>options.fn()</code>. Useful
      when a template must emit mustache syntax it should <em>not</em> expand (docs,
      code samples). Here <code>rawloud</code> upper-cases the raw body, so the verbatim
      <code>{t("{{bar}}")}</code> comes out as <code>{t("{{BAR}}")}</code>. The head
      <b>must</b> name a defined operation — a raw block exists only to feed one, so an
      undefined head is a hard error, never a silent empty render.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.rawBlock.template} data={ex.rawBlock.data} helpers={ex.rawBlock.helpers} />

    <h3>External partials</h3>
    <p>
      MaxBars resolves <b>external</b> <code>{t("{{> name}}")}</code> against host-supplied partials —
      each itself MaxBars source, so a partial uses the full surface (pipes, infix) — in both the
      interpreter (<code>renderMaxbarsWithPartials</code>) and the compiler
      (<code>compileMaxbarsWithPartials</code>), exactly like <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a>.
      Combined with <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> it templates a row each, the name resolvable from
      the data (<code>{t("{{> (lookup this \"kind\")}}")}</code>). A template-local
      <code>{t("{{#inline}}")}</code> of the same name wins; only the <code>{t("{{#*inline}}")}</code>
      <em>decorator</em> stays a FullBars job (see <a href="#divergences">where it diverges</a>).
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.partials.template} data={ex.partials.data} partials={ex.partials.partials} />
    <h3>Layout partials &amp; <code>{t("{{yield}}")}</code></h3>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{#inline \"x\"}}…{{yield}}…{{/inline}}")}</code> defines a layout with a hole, and
      <code>{t("{{#partial \"x\"}}body{{/partial}}")}</code> invokes it — the definition drops
      <code>body</code> in at <code>{t("{{yield}}")}</code>, MaxBars' bare spelling of Handlebars'
      <code>{t("{{> @partial-block}}")}</code>.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.layoutPartials.template} data={ex.layoutPartials.data} />
    <h3>Custom helpers</h3>
    <p>
      MaxBars reuses FullBars's engine, so a host registers operations with the same
      <code>registerHelper(name, fn)</code> at the JS boundary (native MaxBars calls these
      operations/definitions, <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}adr/adr-0019-operation-vocabulary/`}>ADR-019</a>):
      <code>loud</code> (inline), <code>link</code> (hash args), and <code>list</code> (a block
      helper binding the drop-pipes <code>{t("as p i")}</code>).
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.helpers.template} data={ex.helpers.data} helpers={ex.helpers.helpers} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────── Putting it together ─────────────────────── -->
  <section id="together">
    <h2>Putting it together</h2>
    <p>
      Real templates reuse these pieces at once. A plain-text shipping notice —
      interpolation, a dotted path, an <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> with its
      <code>{t("{{else}}")}</code>, and an external <code>{t("{{> item}}")}</code> partial:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.email.template} data={ex.email.data} partials={ex.email.partials} />
    <h3>When the output <em>is</em> HTML</h3>
    <p>
      The capstone is an HTML card: a <code>{t("{{> styles}}")}</code> partial holds the CSS once,
      <code>{t("{{> card}}")}</code> is one row's markup with a <code>{t("{{#if lead}}")}</code> badge,
      and <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> iterates. (The one example that previews as HTML in the Lab.)
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.card.template} data={ex.card.data} partials={ex.card.partials} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────── Where MaxBars diverges ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="divergences">
    <h2>Where MaxBars diverges from FullBars</h2>
    <p>
      The expression layer costs a few things FullBars has. Each is a direct
      consequence of putting operators and a pipe into the grammar — none is a
      surprise once you see why.
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>FullBars</th><th>MaxBars</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Bar in a block head <span class="code">{t("{{#each xs | f}}")}</span> / <span class="code">{t("as |x|")}</span></td>
          <td>a <b>parse error</b></td>
          <td>MaxBars block params <b>drop the pipes</b> (<span class="code">{t("as x")}</span>), so a bar in a head is rejected — whether it was meant as the Handlebars block-param delimiter or an unparenthesised pipe. To pipe a head argument, parenthesise it: <span class="code">{t("{{#each (xs | f) as x}}")}</span>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Block partials + <span class="code">{t("{{yield}}")}</span></td>
          <td>bare <span class="code">{t("{{#inline}}")}</span></td>
          <td>Inline-defined partials work — <span class="code">{t("{{#inline \"layout\"}}<{{yield}}>{{/inline}}")}</span> then <span class="code">{t("{{#partial \"layout\"}}…{{/partial}}")}</span> — where <span class="code">{t("{{yield}}")}</span> renders the caller's block body (MaxBars' bare spelling of Handlebars' <span class="code">{t("{{> @partial-block}}")}</span>; the <span class="code">@</span> sigil is reserved, so that Handlebars form is rejected). Host-threaded <em>external</em> partials (<span class="code">{t("{{> name}}")}</span>) work too, render and compile. Only the <span class="code">{t("{{#*inline}}")}</span> <em>decorator</em> stays FullBars-only — MaxBars uses bare <span class="code">{t("{{#inline}}")}</span>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Infix anywhere</td>
          <td>not in a <b>clause separator</b></td>
          <td><span class="code">{t("{{else if (gt n 0)}}")}</span> must parenthesise. The bare form <span class="code">{t("{{else if n > 0}}")}</span> parses but silently takes the wrong branch.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>JS-flavoured <span class="code">+</span></td>
          <td>arithmetic is <b>strictly numeric</b></td>
          <td><span class="code">{t("\"a\" + \"b\"")}</span> throws — there is no string concatenation operator.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Names with <span class="code">+ - * / % ? :</span> (e.g. <span class="code">{t("{{first-name}}")}</span>, <span class="code">{t("{{done?}}")}</span>)</td>
          <td>need <b>[bracket] segments</b></td>
          <td>Those are operator characters here, so <span class="code">{t("{{first-name}}")}</span> reads as subtraction and <span class="code">{t("{{done?}}")}</span> as a ternary head. Bracket the key — <span class="code">{t("{{[first-name]}}")}</span>, <span class="code">{t("{{[done?]}}")}</span> — the same segment-literal escape <a href="/fullbars#identifiers">FullBars</a> uses. (In FullBars and Mustache these are ordinary name characters.)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mustache-style <span class="code">{t("{{^x}}")}</span> / <span class="code">{t("{{&x}}")}</span> / <span class="code">{t("{{{{raw}}}}")}</span></td>
          <td>rejected</td>
          <td>MaxBars sets <span class="code">extras = false</span> — it's not the Handlebars-compatibility dialect.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="tip">
      The separator rule is the one real trap: a bare infix condition in
      <code>{t("{{else if …}}")}</code> doesn't error, it just renders wrong. Always
      wrap it — as the <a href="#operators">conditionals example</a> does
      (<code>{t("{{#if stock >= 10}}")}</code> infix, but <code>{t("{{else if (gte stock 1)}}")}</code>
      parenthesised).
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────────── Compiles to JS ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="compile">
    <h2>Compiles to JS</h2>
    <p>
      Like the other surfaces, MaxBars <b>compiles to a standalone JS module</b>, and
      FlatBars gates that the compiled module and the interpreter produce
      <b>byte-identical</b> output — operators and pipes included, since they desugar
      before compilation. Expand <b>Compiled JS</b> on the loop example:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.eachList.template} data={ex.eachList.data} compile />
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────── When (not) to use MaxBars ─────────────────── -->
  <section id="when">
    <h2>When (not) to use MaxBars</h2>
    <p>
      MaxBars is the right surface when the <b>view genuinely needs expressions</b> —
      formatting, thresholds, small computed values — and you'd rather write
      <code>{t("{{price * qty | toFixed 2}}")}</code> than thread a pre-computed field
      through your model for every cell.
    </p>
    <p>Reach for a sibling instead when:</p>
    <ul class="conv">
      <li>You want <b>strict model-view separation</b> — push all logic into the model and keep templates dumb. That's <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a> (or, outside this family, StringTemplate).</li>
      <li>You want <b>Handlebars compatibility</b> — block params, partials, the <code>{t("{{&x}}")}</code> spellings. That's <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a>.</li>
      <li>You're porting <b>Mustache</b> templates. That's <a href="/minbars">MinBars</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Same engine, same compiler, same ~80-helper <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}engine/prelude/`}>prelude</a>
      under all four — MaxBars just turns the most sugar on. Jump into the
      <a href="/lab/index.html?engine=maxbars">Lab</a> and start typing, or read the
      normative <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}engine/maxbars/`}>maxbars.adoc</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Reference>
